course: religion-ethics
lesson: 69
title: When grown-ups grieve, and how it gets softer
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grown-ups grieve too: a month after Grandad died, Ellie hears her mum crying in the bathroom, because Grandad was Mum's own dad, and nobody is ever too old to miss their mum or dad.
explain: Sometimes grown-ups say «I'm fine» to protect children, even when they are not; a child can knock gently, give a hug, or say «you don't have to pretend for me», without having to fix anything.
explain: Grief usually gets softer with time: Oscar's grandad says that a year after his wife died the pain is «like a bruise that's healing», still there when touched, but no longer sharp all the time.
explain: Getting help is part of healing: Ben's mum talks to a grief counsellor, Holly's aunt finds comfort in her church, Charlie's grandad joins a lunch club, and Lily's dad slowly starts laughing again, which is healing, not forgetting.
explain: Christians believe God comforts all who mourn and slowly turns mourning into joy; people of every faith and none find that love, friends and time help.
explain: A photo that once made everyone cry, like Jayden's grandad in the hallway, can one day make them smile: that is how grief changes into thankful love.
fact: Grown-ups grieve too, sometimes for a long time.
fact: Being grown-up does not stop you missing your own mum or dad.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes hide sadness to protect children; families often heal better when they can be sad together.
fact: For most people the pain of grief gets softer over time; it does not vanish completely, but there is more room for happy memories.
fact: A grief counsellor is someone trained to help people with big feelings after a death.
fact: Many churches run bereavement support groups; people of all beliefs use counsellors and helplines.
fact: Getting help with grief is brave, not weak.
fact: Laughing and being happy again does not mean forgetting or not loving the person who died.
fact: Making new friends after a loss, for example at a lunch club, helps people feel less lonely; it does not replace the person who died.
fact: People can miss someone their whole life, but after many years the missing is usually gentler.
fact: Christians believe God comforts those who mourn; Jesus said, «Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.»
fact: You do not have to forget someone in order to heal.
exercise: Why is Ellie's mum crying in the bathroom a month after Grandad died? | Grandad was her own dad, and she misses him | She is cross with Ellie about something | Grown-ups only cry when they are hurt | She has a cold and her eyes are watering
exercise: What can Ellie do when she hears her mum crying? | Knock gently and offer a hug or «I love you» | Barge straight in and tell her firmly to stop crying now | Pretend she heard nothing ever again | Phone the police straight away
exercise: Does Ellie have to fix her mum's sadness? | No; being there with a hug is enough | Yes; children must cheer parents up | Yes; she must find a way to stop it | No, because grown-ups don't really feel sad
exercise: Tom asks why his dad is so sad about his own mum dying, when Dad's a grown-up. What is true? | Everyone, at any age, can miss their mum or dad | Grown-ups only pretend to be sad at funerals | Dad should be over it by now, because he is old and grown-up | Only children really love their parents
exercise: Mia's mum says «It's okay, I'm fine», but Mia can tell she's not. Why might Mum say it? | To protect Mia from her sadness | Because grown-ups never feel sad | Because she wants Mia to go away | Because Mia did something wrong
exercise: What could Mia kindly say to her mum? | «You don't have to pretend for me» | «Stop being so sad, Mum, it's getting annoying» | «Grown-ups aren't allowed to cry, are they?» | «I'll be sad instead of you, so you can stop»
exercise: Oscar's grandad says the pain is «softer now, like a bruise that's healing». What does he mean? | It still hurts when touched, but less sharply | He hurt his arm falling over at the funeral and it's mending now | The pain has gone completely and for good | It hurts more and more every year
exercise: Why does Ben's mum see a grief counsellor? | A trained person can help with big feelings | Because she has done something wrong and must be told off | Because counsellors can bring people back to say goodbye | Because the law makes all widows go to one every week
exercise: What is getting help with grief? | Brave, not weak | Weak and embarrassing | Only for very old people | Against most religions
exercise: Lily feels angry that her dad has started laughing again. What is true? | Laughing again is healing, not forgetting Mum | Dad has stopped loving Mum completely | Dad should stay sad for the rest of his life, every single day | Laughing is disrespectful to the dead
exercise: Charlie's grandad joins a lunch club and makes new friends. Is that okay? | Yes; new friends help, they don't replace Grandma | No; he should stay at home alone to show how much he loved her | No; it means he has forgotten Grandma | Yes, because Grandma was a bad wife
exercise: Freddie's teacher still misses her mum, ten years after she died. What is true? | People can miss someone all their life, more gently | Ten years means she must be pretending | Missing someone should stop after one year, or something is wrong | Teachers aren't allowed to have feelings
exercise: Holly's aunt goes back to church after her husband died. How did it help her? | People there cared for her and she prayed | The vicar told her to stop missing him | Church made her forget all about her husband and the sad times | Church gave her a new husband
exercise: What helps people of no religion who are grieving? | Friends, family, groups and counsellors | Nothing at all; only religion can help with grief | Pretending to everyone that nobody has died | Staying right away from other people for a while
exercise: Why does Jayden's family now smile at Grandad's photo? | Over time grief turned into thankful love | They have forgotten who is in the photo | The photo has been changed to a funny one | They were never really sad about him
exercise: What did Jesus say about those who mourn? | «Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted» | «Those who mourn must hide their tears» | «Mourning is a sin against God» | «Only children are allowed to mourn»
test: «Me mam keeps sayin' she's grand but she's not grand at all,» says a girl in Dublin (grand = fine). Best thing to do? | Gently tell Mum she doesn't have to pretend | Believe her and never ask her about it again, ever | Tell everyone at school Mum's lying | Get angry with Mum for hiding things
test: «Dad's like 40, why's he bawling about Gran, he's well old,» says Tom's brother (bawling = crying loudly). What's true? | You never get too old to miss your mum | Old people don't feel real sadness like kids do | Dad is only crying to get attention from Gran's friends | Dad should be well over it by now, at his age
test: The vicar says, very formally: «The congregation extends its sympathy and will hold the family in its prayers.» What is he offering? | The church's care and prayers for the family | A list of rules for the funeral | A bill for the funeral service | A promise that grief will stop soon
test: «My dad's acting all happy again, that's mad disrespectful to Mum, fr» (fr = for real), says Lily. What's the wisest answer? | Being happy again is healing; Dad still loves Mum | She's right; Dad has forgotten Mum | Dad should be told off by the whole family for it | Happy people never really loved anyone in the first place
test: What would happen to most people's grief over a few years? | It would usually get softer, with more happy memories | It would always get stronger and sharper as the years went by | It would vanish completely in one day | It would turn into anger for ever
test: A Gen X uncle says, «Grandpa joining a lunch club? Bummer, guess he's over Grandma» (bummer = shame). Put him right. | New friends ease loneliness; they don't replace Grandma | He's right; Grandpa has forgotten her and moved on with new friends | Grandpa is breaking a church rule | Grandpa should stay home alone
test: «Mum's seeing a counsellor? She's not cray cray,» says Ben's cousin (cray cray = crazy). What's true? | Counsellors help ordinary people with big feelings | Only very ill people in hospital ever see counsellors | Counsellors make you forget the dead | Seeing a counsellor is shameful
test: In a TV show, a widow says, «I'll never smile again», and the show ends there. What happens for most real people? | Grief softens and smiles slowly come back | They really never smile again, not once | They forget their husband within a week | They must move far away to a new town to be happy
test: «Is it gonna hurt like this forever?» asks Oscar. Which answer is honest? | It usually gets softer, though you may always miss her | Yes, exactly like this, for ever and ever, every day | No, by tomorrow you'll feel nothing | Only if you don't go to church every day to pray
test: A girl in Cardiff says, «Our Nan's been tamping since Bampi died» (tamping = furious, Bampi = Grandad). What's going on? | Anger can be part of grief for grown-ups too | Nan is angry with the children for being noisy | Nan never really liked Bampi much, even before | Grown-ups only ever get angry, never sad like kids
test: Myth to check: «If you stop crying, it means you've forgotten them.» | False; you can heal without forgetting | True; tears are the only proof of love | True; healing means forgetting | False, but only after ten years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises grief in adults? | Adults experience grief, at times for prolonged periods, as do children | Adults are exempt from grief by reason of maturity | Adults grieve exclusively for their own children | Adults conclude their grief upon the day of the funeral
exam: Identify the principal function of a grief counsellor. | To assist bereaved persons in coping with powerful emotions | To restore the deceased to life by consultation | To determine the inheritance of the deceased's estate | To compel the bereaved to forget the deceased
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the course of grief over time. | It commonly diminishes in intensity while affection and memory endure | It invariably increases in severity with each passing year | It ceases entirely and abruptly after one calendar month | It may be concluded only by forgetting the deceased
exam: It is given that a widowed parent resumes laughter some months after a bereavement. Ascertain which inference is consistent with the matters taught. | The parent is healing and has not ceased to love the deceased | The parent has forgotten the deceased entirely | The parent is acting contrary to every religious tradition | The parent was never attached to the deceased
